.TH DOTDOC 1 "August 2026" "dotdoc @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dotdoc \- scan a directory tree for symbolic-link findings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dotdoc
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dotdoc
scans a directory tree recursively and reports broken and absolute symbolic links.

Dotfiles are the primary use case, but an explicitly specified
.IR PATH
may be any directory tree.

Without
.IR PATH ,
the directory
.B $HOME/dotfiles
is scanned.

With
.IR PATH ,
the specified directory tree is scanned.

Directory symbolic links are inspected as symbolic links but their target
directories are not traversed.
.PP
.B \-\-exclude
omits matching scan-root-relative entries from the scan and applies to
both broken and absolute symbolic-link findings.
.PP
.B \-\-max\-depth
limits how deep the scan descends.
The scan root is depth 0, and a direct entry is depth 1.
.PP
.B \-\-only
filters diagnostic findings by kind after the scan completes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATH"
Exclude
.I PATH
relative to the scan root.
This option may be repeated.
.IP
.I PATH
is matched as a literal path after lexical normalization.
It is not a glob, regular expression, or ignore-file rule.
The path is not canonicalized, and symbolic-link targets are not followed
when deciding whether an entry is excluded.
.IP
If
.I PATH
names a directory, that directory and its subtree are pruned during
traversal.
If
.I PATH
names a file or symbolic link, that entry is skipped on exact match.
.B \-\-exclude .\&
and any
.I PATH
that lexically normalizes to
.B .\&
exclude the entire scan root.
.IP
A nonexistent exclude path is ignored.
An empty
.IR PATH ,
an absolute
.IR PATH ,
or a
.I PATH
that lexically escapes the scan root is an invocation error and causes
.B dotdoc
to exit with status 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " N"
Scan through depth
.IR N .
The scan root is depth 0, and a direct entry is depth 1.
This option may be repeated.
The last value is used.
.IP
.B \-\-max\-depth 0
validates the scan root but does not scan any entries.
.B \-\-max\-depth
.I N
diagnoses through depth
.I N
and does not descend deeper.
.IP
.I N
must be a non-negative integer.
This option may be combined with
.BR \-\-exclude .
An invalid
.I N
is an invocation error and causes
.B dotdoc
to exit with status 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " KIND"
Show only findings of diagnostic
.IR KIND .
The available kinds are
.B broken
and
.BR absolute .
This option may be repeated.
Repeated values use OR semantics.
.IP
All diagnostic findings are collected before this filter is applied.
Output, the reported finding count, and exit status are based on the
filtered findings.
If no findings remain after filtering,
.B dotdoc
prints
.B "OK: no findings."
and exits with status 0.
An unknown
.I KIND
is an invocation error and causes
.B dotdoc
to exit with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
Diagnostic findings are written to standard output, one line per finding.
After the finding lines,
.B dotdoc
prints a one-line summary containing the total finding count and the
per-kind counts.
.PP
Kind counts are always shown in the fixed order
.B BROKEN
then
.BR ABSOLUTE ,
including kinds whose count is zero.
When
.B \-\-only
is used, the total and per-kind counts are based only on the filtered findings.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Found 2 findings (BROKEN: 1, ABSOLUTE: 1).
.RE
.fi

Invocation and filesystem errors are written to standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scan completed successfully with no findings.
.TP
.B 1
The scan completed successfully and diagnostic findings were found.
This does not indicate program failure.
.TP
.B 2
An invocation, filesystem, or scan error occurred.
Invalid
.BR \-\-exclude ,
.BR \-\-max\-depth ,
or
.B \-\-only
usage is an invocation error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan the default dotfiles tree:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc
.RE
.fi
.PP
Scan a specific directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc ~/dotfiles
.RE
.fi
.PP
Skip known-noise subtrees under a broad scan root:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc --exclude .local/share/Steam --exclude .cache "$HOME"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Limit scan depth under a broad scan root:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc --max-depth 3 "$HOME"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Show only broken symbolic-link findings:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc --only broken "$HOME/dotfiles"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Select multiple diagnostic kinds with OR semantics:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc --only broken --only absolute "$HOME/dotfiles"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Display help:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotdoc --help
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $HOME/dotfiles
Default scan root when no
.I PATH
is specified.
